<b>Question: What is meant by the term "representation" as used in
 the phrase "schema is a network of representations"?</b><p>

<b>Answer</b>: Representations are "signs" of something else, they
stand for the original thing. For example, a photo is a representation
of a state of reality.  In a sense, representations are "mental
pictures" of stimuli, events, states. etc.  Now, those pictures are
not literally visual, but it's easier to think about it that way.<p>
	  The mind creates representations during perception and
sometimes keeps them in memory for a long time.  What's special about
representations that are part of a schema is that they can represent
(stand for) not only events in the external world but also internal
events.  For example, if your body is in some significant
physiological or emotional state, the mind can form a representation
of that state and under some conditions keep a "blueprint" of it in
memory.  Next time this representation is activated, the blueprint
somehow reconstructs at least parts of the original emotional state.
<p> <hr>

<b>Question: What is the main difference between intention and
intentionality?</b>  <p>

<b>Answer</b>: Intention is a <i>mental state</i> that somebody has
before acting (e.g., I intend to send off an email to my department
head in a second).<p>

Intentionality is the quality of an <i>action</i> when it was done
"intentionally" or "on purpose."  So after I send off the email, I
might say that I intentionally sent my department head an email.  Or,
if I accidentally hit the SEND button, I might say that I didn't send
it off intentionally.  <p>

Importantly, intentions can exist without action (I can intend to send
an email without ever doing it), and the action that follows the
intention may not be intentional (I could accidentally send the email
to a student rather than my department head).  However, on the reverse
side, if you do something intentionally, you did have an intention to
do it.<p>
