<b>Question: (Folk Theory of Mind and Behavior) In class you said
that the actor does not know if they have the skill to carry out a
particular behavior but the observer knows if the actor has the skill
or not? I am not quite clear on this.</b> <p>

<b>Answer</b>: There are cases when the observer's judgment of the
actor's skill runs counter to the actor's own judgment of his or her
skill. Because skill is not a subjective state (and because the actor
may be biased in ascribing too much skill to him- or herself), the
observer's opinion often has more weight.  Consider the case of the
darts player in the Malle & Knobe paper.  Other people (our research
subjects) who watch the dispute between the actor (who claims he hit
the triple-20 intentionally, with skill) and his friend (who claims he
hit the triple-20 unintentionally, with luck) side with the friend.
This is not an issue about who is "truly" right about the judgment of
skill but an issue of what happens in social situations -- where
people are sometimes skeptical of the actor's own claims of skill.
<p>
