<b>Question: We were not very clear what mere behaviors are.  Why
can we not call any behavior a mere behavior?</b><p>

<b>Answer</b>: We could call everything that goes on inside and on
people's bodies a "behavioral event" (to distinguish them from events
in the world).  We could call all observable behavioral events
"behaviors" (to distinguish them from mental states, which are
unobservable).  But then we still have to distinguish between
intentional and unintentional behaviors -- and that's where the labels
"action" vs. "mere behavior" comes into play.  The exact labels are
not very important, but the underlying distinctions are.
