<b>Question: What is personality?</b>
<p>
<b>Answer</b>: The definition depends very much on a researcher's
theoretical position, but a working definition might be: Personality
is the sum of those relatively enduring attributes that differentiate a person
from other people.  

<p>
<hr>
<b>Question: What is a disposition?</b>

<p> <b>Answer</b>: A property of an organism that makes the organism
tend to behave a certain way when certain stimuli are present.  Just
like sugar has the disposition to dissolve when put in water, so do
some people have the disposition to, for example, get excited (or
nervous) when in the presence of other people. 
<p>
<hr>
<b>Question: What is the difference between self-concept and
self-schema?</b>
<p>
<b>Answer</b>: The self-concept refers to the entire set of thoughts,
feelings, memories that a person has about him- or herself.  A
self-schema refers to only a small part of the self-concept -- a part
that is elaborate and consists of tightly interconnected elements.  A
self-schema always refers to a particular domain (e.g., once artistic
abilities, once extraversion, one's romantic relationships).

<p>
<hr>
<b>Question: What does the label "dispositionist" mean?</b>
<p>
<b>Answer</b>: A "dispositionist" believes in the reality of traits
(dispositions) and believes that personality traits both cause and explain
behavior patterns.  Contrary to that, a "situationist" does not
believe in the reality of traits and does not believe that personality
traits cause or explain behavior.  (Most situationists concede that
<i>some</i> traits may exists, such as intelligence or optimism, but
they would argue that traits cause or explain behavior to a much
smaller degree than situations do.)

<hr> <b>Question: Does affective aggression include intent to harm
others?  Also, when you speak of heritability of 20-30%, does that
refer to environmental factors or genetic factors? Could you explain
again how twins are related in terms of aggression? Do they
share a similar tendency towards aggression?</b>
<p>
<b>Answer</b>:Yes, affective aggression includes the intent to harm others.  It
doesn't have to be fully deliberated and thought out in detail, just
intended at the time it happens.  
<p>
Heritability of 20-30% refers to the amount of genetic factors that
underlie variations in aggression from person to person.   Twins,
especially monozygotic twins, will show substantial similarity in
aggression due to the shared genes but also due to their shared
environment (both parents and surroundings).   


<hr> <b>Question</b>: What is the difference between the peripheral route
of persuasion and heuristics?
<p>
<b>Answer</b>: The peripheral route <i>makes use of</i> people's
heuristic information processing.  Heuristic processing relies on
well-learned, automatic, sometimes unconscious response patterns
(e.g., to authority, to credible sources, to sexual or affective cues,
to tone of voice, color, etc.). 

<hr> <b>Question</b>: What is meant by "power asymmetries"? (in the
lecture on prejudice) <p> <b>Answer</b>: One source of stereotypes (or
prejudice) can be a power asymmetry between two groups (think of all
the ethnic and religious conflicts all over the world).  These power
asymmetries clearly mark the difference between groups, thus allowing
reliable categorization and opening the door for stereotyping. For
example, some members of the more powerful group (e.g., the one who
rules the country or holds the wealth or controls natural resources)
may act in discriminatory ways, and the less powerful group will
likely adopt the stereotype that every member of the powerful group is
discriminatory.  Some members of the less powerful group may appear
aggressive (just think of protesters in the street!), and the powerful
group will likely adopt the stereotype that all members of the other
group are aggressive and destructive.
