<b>Question</b>: Right now I am studying persuasion and compliance.  I
am trying to gain a better understanding about the central and
peripheral route to persuasive influence.  One problem I am having in
conceptualizing the two is your use of the terms "transparent" and
"non-transparent".  Are these like overt and covert or implicit and
explicit, or perhaps conscious and unconscious?  Also can I use the
terms systematic processing and heuristic processing synonymously with
the central route and peripheral route?<p>


<b>Answer</b>: <I>Transparent</I> refers to an influence tactic that
the target recognizes as an an influence tactic.   Transparent
influence tactics will typically lead to conscious (central)
processing.<br>
	The central/peripheral route distinction refers to the
persuader's choice of tactics; the systematic/heuristic processing
distinction refers to the target's way of taking in the persuasion.
Most of the time, central-route persuasion goes along with systematic
processing and peripheral-route persuasion with heuristic processing,
but there are of course exceptions (e.g., you may systematically
dissect an emotion-triggering commercial).
